1
00:00:12,410 --> 00:00:18,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, we got a question this morning, and I was wondering if you could answer it again.

2
00:00:20,653 --> 00:00:24,614
[SPEAKER_01]: And the question is, what is the difference between chanda and taṇhā?

3
00:00:27,095 --> 00:00:32,616
[SPEAKER_01]: Chanda being desire, taṇhā being craving.

4
00:00:36,758 --> 00:00:37,038
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

5
00:00:37,138 --> 00:00:41,259
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the difference is that chanda,

6
00:00:43,033 --> 00:00:53,009
[SPEAKER_00]: As a desire, as a form of zeal, interest, can... well, can be either wholesome or unwholesome.

7
00:00:54,327 --> 00:01:02,950
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you have a desire towards sensuality, that is kāma, chanda, chanda rāga, that is not a wholesome desire.

8
00:01:03,711 --> 00:01:19,437
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, if you want to be free from sensuality, say, you started and you began to understand the dissatisfaction of it and so on, then you can actually have a desire to be free from it, to be free from sensuality and sensual desire.

9
00:01:19,657 --> 00:01:20,998
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's still a form of desire,

10
00:01:21,915 --> 00:01:26,698
[SPEAKER_00]: That can now make you actually want to practice the dhamma and sense restraint and so on.

11
00:01:27,318 --> 00:01:28,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's also desire.

12
00:01:29,400 --> 00:01:33,582
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's a desire that can actually, eventually, result in freedom from desire.

13
00:01:34,102 --> 00:01:40,306
[SPEAKER_00]: Freedom from sensual desire, freedom from the desire for pleasures and so on.

14
00:01:41,487 --> 00:01:44,609
[SPEAKER_00]: So, chanda is not in itself

15
00:01:46,431 --> 00:01:47,872
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not bad, no good.

16
00:01:48,212 --> 00:01:51,334
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really... it depends where you direct it.

17
00:01:52,095 --> 00:01:58,879
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why the... those, like, types of striving that the Buddha describes in the suttas, you know, that's all based on desire.

18
00:01:59,159 --> 00:02:04,983
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire for abandoning the unwholesome states and desire for increasing the wholesome states.

19
00:02:07,508 --> 00:02:12,292
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not a question of not having desires when you begin to practice.

20
00:02:13,093 --> 00:02:19,098
[SPEAKER_00]: It's really about having the desires that are directed at development of wholesomeness and skillfulness.

21
00:02:19,897 --> 00:02:25,501
[SPEAKER_00]: That that will then result in diminishing of unwholesome and unwholesome desires and everything else.

22
00:02:26,221 --> 00:02:36,288
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, when you're completely free from any desire towards sensuality and so on, you will also lose the desire towards wholesomeness, because that is now what you are.

23
00:02:36,688 --> 00:02:40,911
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you don't need to have a desire to develop wholesomeness, once you develop the wholesomeness.

24
00:02:41,832 --> 00:02:49,117
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's how desire, directed rightly, can result in freedom from desire altogether, freedom from chanda altogether.

25
00:02:51,862 --> 00:02:59,107
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could say that the arahant, the one accomplished, fully accomplished, the Buddha, has no desire.

26
00:02:59,788 --> 00:03:01,609
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, he's free from chanda completely.

27
00:03:01,649 --> 00:03:02,229
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

28
00:03:02,910 --> 00:03:04,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning desire is not a matter of choice.

29
00:03:05,632 --> 00:03:13,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Abandoning of desire, it's a matter of directing desire towards the behavior of a certain kind, i.e.

30
00:03:14,158 --> 00:03:19,882
[SPEAKER_00]: gradual training, that will then remove any basis for perpetuation of desire later on.

31
00:03:20,722 --> 00:03:21,503
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words,

32
00:03:23,556 --> 00:03:24,996
[SPEAKER_00]: You become aware of your illness.

33
00:03:25,917 --> 00:03:38,319
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to have a desire to be cured, because that desire will make you seek for... look for a doctor, find the right medicine, and then sustain the application of the medicine on the basis of desire for health.

34
00:03:39,379 --> 00:03:50,782
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, once you're cured and healed, and there is absolutely no more... not even, like, the slightest traces of your illness, you will have no more desire left to be cured, because you're cured.

35
00:03:52,521 --> 00:04:06,130
[SPEAKER_00]: But, if while you're ill, you think that, okay, so being free from illness means no desire, thus I will have no desire, that just will prevent you from actually curing yourself.

36
00:04:06,170 --> 00:04:10,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to direct the desire rightly, on account of which you will start making the right effort.

37
00:04:10,753 --> 00:04:13,355
[SPEAKER_00]: A person cannot distinguish in the beginning desire.

38
00:04:14,196 --> 00:04:14,956
[SPEAKER_00]: What is this desire?

39
00:04:14,976 --> 00:04:15,557
[SPEAKER_00]: What is else?

40
00:04:15,577 --> 00:04:18,118
[SPEAKER_00]: Desire is bad, desire is bad.

41
00:04:18,679 --> 00:04:18,919
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

42
00:04:20,756 --> 00:04:23,200
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you need to start with a sense of strength, as we always say.

43
00:04:24,541 --> 00:04:28,046
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't want to be trying to not have desires.

44
00:04:28,747 --> 00:04:31,671
[SPEAKER_00]: You want to be trying to see where your desires are directed at.

45
00:04:32,653 --> 00:04:35,317
[SPEAKER_00]: And if it's a desire for physical pleasures,

46
00:04:36,906 --> 00:04:44,912
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, of the lustful kind, where we spoke before, the one that incites the lust and overwhelms the mind and makes you get out of the chair, as we gave the simile.

47
00:04:46,373 --> 00:04:48,034
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the desire you should not cultivate.

48
00:04:48,555 --> 00:04:55,920
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the desire you should abandon, not welcome, not entertain, endure until it passes without acting out of.

49
00:04:57,762 --> 00:05:03,006
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the question of, say for example, anāgāmī, who is free from sensual desire, he's not free from chanda.

50
00:05:03,995 --> 00:05:14,179
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, freedom from sensuality doesn't mean absence of chanda of any kind.

51
00:05:14,319 --> 00:05:19,420
[SPEAKER_00]: It means loss of interest, loss of zeal for the sensual type of pleasure.

52
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:20,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

53
00:05:20,441 --> 00:05:27,063
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because the gratification has been fully understood, the danger has been fully understood, and the escape has been fully understood.

54
00:05:28,060 --> 00:05:33,968
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not that anāgāmī or an arahant have no concept anymore of what physical pleasure would feel like.

55
00:05:34,589 --> 00:05:40,436
[SPEAKER_00]: It's they have no interest in it anymore on the level of their mind and intent and thought.

56
00:05:40,536 --> 00:05:42,599
[SPEAKER_00]: So they're absolutely free from any zeal

57
00:05:43,580 --> 00:05:46,643
[SPEAKER_00]: that person not free from sensuality would otherwise have.

58
00:05:46,843 --> 00:05:47,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And you could see that.

59
00:05:47,543 --> 00:06:01,895
[SPEAKER_00]: You have a... you would have like a person who have a sensual thought and there is immediate like quite a strong, depending on how much mind development you have sort of engaged with, but there is... there will be an inclination towards it.

60
00:06:01,915 --> 00:06:06,900
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be like this this welcoming of that prospect, even if you practice sense restraint.

61
00:06:07,500 --> 00:06:09,924
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you're not free from sensuality.

62
00:06:10,425 --> 00:06:16,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Because of that mental delight, chanda, desire, zeal, whatever you want to translate it.

63
00:06:17,858 --> 00:06:20,102
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one... and that can only be abandoned

64
00:06:21,018 --> 00:06:32,330
[SPEAKER_00]: through seeing the danger of that which you're delighting towards, that which you're... because if you're... you know, you will not have an interest anymore in ingesting poison, if you understood the deadliness of the poison.

65
00:06:33,151 --> 00:06:34,933
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the texture of it hasn't changed.

66
00:06:35,013 --> 00:06:38,978
[SPEAKER_00]: The physical contact, pleasure... of a pleasurable kind hasn't changed.

67
00:06:39,904 --> 00:06:43,727
[SPEAKER_00]: But your understanding now, of seeing it as a poison, that has changed.

68
00:06:43,767 --> 00:06:48,250
[SPEAKER_00]: So you have absolutely no zeal towards that kind of pleasure whatsoever.

69
00:06:48,810 --> 00:06:49,951
[SPEAKER_00]: You have no interest in it.

70
00:06:50,031 --> 00:06:54,694
[SPEAKER_01]: That's... you know, a lot of people can say, well, I understand that sensuality is bad.

71
00:06:55,081 --> 00:06:55,761
[SPEAKER_01]: I understand that.

72
00:06:55,801 --> 00:06:56,282
[SPEAKER_01]: I get that.

73
00:06:56,362 --> 00:06:56,722
[SPEAKER_01]: I get it.

74
00:06:56,882 --> 00:07:00,224
[SPEAKER_01]: I've reflected on the dangers, yet I'm still attracted.

75
00:07:00,724 --> 00:07:01,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

76
00:07:01,204 --> 00:07:02,225
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the Buddha said that.

77
00:07:02,505 --> 00:07:14,772
[SPEAKER_00]: For as long as you are not... for as long as you haven't fully understood the gratification, fully understood the danger, and fully understood the escape, you should not claim that you understood sensuality.

78
00:07:15,767 --> 00:07:18,029
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you do, means you're contradicting yourself.

79
00:07:18,529 --> 00:07:24,674
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you fully did understand these three aspects, there will be no chanda towards sensuality left.

80
00:07:24,694 --> 00:07:30,318
[SPEAKER_00]: There will be chanda towards renunciation, towards seclusion, towards further abandonment.

81
00:07:31,099 --> 00:07:32,119
[SPEAKER_00]: And that chanda is fine.

82
00:07:34,001 --> 00:07:36,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it will make you make more of the effort.

83
00:07:37,808 --> 00:07:41,671
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yes, a person will inevitably think, yeah, I understand the peril of sensuality.

84
00:07:41,751 --> 00:07:42,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, fine.

85
00:07:43,232 --> 00:07:44,713
[SPEAKER_00]: Ask yourself then the next question.

86
00:07:45,213 --> 00:08:00,904
[SPEAKER_00]: So, am I free from any form of delight, welcoming, zeal, interest, curiosity even, towards pleasures that come on account of object of the senses that are inducive of lust and so on?

87
00:08:01,165 --> 00:08:02,386
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I don't think I'm free of that.

88
00:08:02,606 --> 00:08:03,927
[SPEAKER_00]: Or I'm not sure if I'm free of that.

89
00:08:03,987 --> 00:08:05,087
[SPEAKER_00]: Means you're not free of that.

90
00:08:05,148 --> 00:08:06,909
[SPEAKER_00]: Means don't think you understood it.

91
00:08:07,977 --> 00:08:09,659
[SPEAKER_00]: Means make more effort.

92
00:08:09,739 --> 00:08:25,376
[SPEAKER_00]: Nobody denies that you will have some insight or some degree of insight into sensuality, once you start restraining and looking, but don't think that's the freedom, up until you know, oh, I am free, I don't want this, this is truly something I have no interest in anymore.

93
00:08:25,576 --> 00:08:26,557
[SPEAKER_00]: Quite the opposite.

94
00:08:28,855 --> 00:08:35,561
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, you might spend most of the time of your day not having interest, but are you still liable to have interest towards sensuality?

95
00:08:36,201 --> 00:08:41,286
[SPEAKER_00]: If so, you're not outside of the domain of that kammabhāva and kammachandra.

96
00:08:43,628 --> 00:08:45,769
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you always want to look on that level of liability.

97
00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:49,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, but am I liable to become careless and interested in this again?

98
00:08:51,014 --> 00:08:51,354
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe.

99
00:08:51,454 --> 00:08:52,075
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, there you go.

100
00:08:52,315 --> 00:08:54,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Not free from doubt in regard to it, not free from it.

101
00:08:56,753 --> 00:09:02,638
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to really abandon that zeal towards the objects of your senses.

102
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:06,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's freedom from karmic chanda.

103
00:09:12,002 --> 00:09:16,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as for taṇhā, if it comes down to taṇhā now, that's... it's... it's subtle.

104
00:09:16,424 --> 00:09:17,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Taṇhā is sort of on a different level.

105
00:09:18,184 --> 00:09:28,688
[SPEAKER_00]: So, chanda is kind of an active thing that you either carelessly engage with on account of your past behavior that hasn't been restrained, or something you can cultivate, as we would say, he generates the desire.

106
00:09:29,008 --> 00:09:36,050
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you reflect why it's good to be cured, why it's good to make the effort, why it's good to practice gradual training, why it's good to be virtuous and sense-restrained.

107
00:09:36,671 --> 00:09:39,211
[SPEAKER_00]: And because of those values you reflect upon, you will

108
00:09:42,167 --> 00:09:49,397
[SPEAKER_00]: Taṇhā, it's more on an immediate level of basically your attitude towards your present enduring feeling, whatever that is.

109
00:09:50,498 --> 00:09:53,121
[SPEAKER_00]: And that attitude is always rooted in resistance.

110
00:09:54,904 --> 00:09:58,689
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is like a taṇhā for pleasure,

111
00:09:59,721 --> 00:10:09,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for pleasure, that is rooted in you resisting the current feeling, which, you know, is not necessarily unpleasant, but it's not sufficient.

112
00:10:10,627 --> 00:10:15,670
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the pleasure, it's not sufficient, which is why you crave for more pleasure, which means, in other words, it's painful.

113
00:10:16,490 --> 00:10:18,171
[SPEAKER_00]: Any resistance is unpleasant.

114
00:10:19,532 --> 00:10:28,036
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you resist, with the mind of resistance, if you experience pleasure, you will, by default, resist it, because, by default, you want more of it.

115
00:10:28,457 --> 00:10:28,977
[SPEAKER_00]: By default.

116
00:10:30,386 --> 00:10:34,269
[SPEAKER_00]: With the mind not free from taṇhā, you experience pain.

117
00:10:34,809 --> 00:10:39,332
[SPEAKER_00]: By default, you're resisting it, because you don't want any of it.

118
00:10:39,412 --> 00:10:40,913
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it completely to go away.

119
00:10:41,834 --> 00:10:52,841
[SPEAKER_00]: So, with the mind not free from taṇhā, you experience a neutral feeling, saying, you will automatically resist it towards oblivion, towards ignorance, towards turning away from it, because you don't understand it.

120
00:10:54,402 --> 00:10:57,445
[SPEAKER_00]: So, taṇhā, practically speaking, is always that resistance

121
00:10:58,586 --> 00:11:00,687
[SPEAKER_00]: on the level of what you're currently feeling.

122
00:11:01,387 --> 00:11:03,248
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're feeling pleasure, you're still resisting.

123
00:11:03,288 --> 00:11:04,808
[SPEAKER_00]: Absolutely, absolutely.

124
00:11:05,028 --> 00:11:09,890
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why, if you're not free from taṇha, every pleasure will be underlined by craving for more.

125
00:11:10,230 --> 00:11:11,471
[SPEAKER_01]: And, yeah, and dukkha.

126
00:11:11,631 --> 00:11:12,431
[SPEAKER_00]: And, exactly.

127
00:11:12,831 --> 00:11:17,673
[SPEAKER_00]: So, as I said, presence of resistance means dukkha.

128
00:11:18,935 --> 00:11:19,716
[SPEAKER_00]: And I said that before.

129
00:11:19,776 --> 00:11:21,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Presence of taṇha means dukkha.

130
00:11:21,637 --> 00:11:22,658
[SPEAKER_00]: Dukkha means taṇha is there.

131
00:11:22,678 --> 00:11:25,700
[SPEAKER_01]: No matter the extent of the pleasure that you can actually get.

132
00:11:26,420 --> 00:11:32,645
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if there is still taṇha there, means there is still resistance to whatever's there for you at the time.

133
00:11:34,246 --> 00:11:35,147
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you suffer.

134
00:11:35,347 --> 00:11:37,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Not because of what's there for you at the time.

135
00:11:39,250 --> 00:11:43,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And why do you, like, why would you be resisting?

136
00:11:44,818 --> 00:11:53,261
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would you... why is it that you are not contented with, you know, some agreeable feeling that is present?

137
00:11:53,281 --> 00:11:56,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it that, by default, the mind wants more of it?

138
00:11:58,583 --> 00:12:06,825
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because of the holding of the view in regard to what pleasure is, what sensuality is, what gratification is, where the value is.

139
00:12:08,626 --> 00:12:12,107
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why sensuality is a form of upādāna.

140
00:12:12,147 --> 00:12:13,167
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a form of assumption.

141
00:12:13,648 --> 00:12:14,588
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a form of a view.

142
00:12:15,706 --> 00:12:18,489
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a, you know, physical experience of pleasure or not.

143
00:12:18,509 --> 00:12:24,914
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically the value that you have mentally, that you uphold through basically, you know, throughout your day-to-day existence.

144
00:12:25,275 --> 00:12:25,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

145
00:12:25,735 --> 00:12:35,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the assumption is that pleasure is... Well, the assumption is that there is no first point, that you were not attracted to centrality and then you began.

146
00:12:35,924 --> 00:12:42,270
[SPEAKER_00]: You start by already being attracted, which means the assumption is that the pleasure is over there.

147
00:12:43,795 --> 00:12:48,136
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason that assumption exists is because you haven't undone, you haven't removed it.

148
00:12:48,176 --> 00:12:49,036
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the sole reason.

149
00:12:49,736 --> 00:12:58,238
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you have to contemplate the danger of your view that pleasure is there, not gratifying it, not welcoming it, not delighting in it.

150
00:12:59,318 --> 00:13:06,499
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the danger of where you think pleasure is will become apparent, and then you would have lost the assumption of where the pleasure is.

151
00:13:07,779 --> 00:13:10,620
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you will have stopped resisting whatever you're feeling currently.

152
00:13:12,038 --> 00:13:13,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Certainly on account of pleasant feeling.

153
00:13:13,559 --> 00:13:19,962
[SPEAKER_00]: You wouldn't be craving for more pleasure, because the only reason you crave for more pleasure is because you keep assuming pleasure to be out there.

154
00:13:20,522 --> 00:13:24,664
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is always, always underlined by craving for more.

155
00:13:24,764 --> 00:13:25,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Unsatisfactory.

156
00:13:25,905 --> 00:13:26,265
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

157
00:13:26,325 --> 00:13:33,889
[SPEAKER_00]: And same then, you will stop resisting by removing that resistance towards insufficiency of pleasure, so to speak, which is your view.

158
00:13:35,089 --> 00:13:36,130
[SPEAKER_00]: It's never quite enough.

159
00:13:37,627 --> 00:13:43,093
[SPEAKER_00]: You will also stop resisting the disagreeability of it, which means disagreeability of anything else.

160
00:13:43,854 --> 00:13:46,096
[SPEAKER_00]: You will not resist it as much, let's say it like that.

161
00:13:47,278 --> 00:13:51,923
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when unpleasant feeling is manifested, you will not, by default, trying to get rid of it.

162
00:13:53,745 --> 00:13:56,808
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what taṇha is.

163
00:13:57,409 --> 00:13:59,531
[SPEAKER_00]: It's that resistance to what's being felt.

164
00:14:00,820 --> 00:14:02,981
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensual pleasure is felt unpleasantly.

165
00:14:03,502 --> 00:14:07,264
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where you want to, you know, seek the outcome.

166
00:14:07,284 --> 00:14:12,227
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where you want to gratify your senses, because non-gratified senses hurt.

167
00:14:13,868 --> 00:14:14,869
[SPEAKER_01]: So you resist the pain.

168
00:14:14,889 --> 00:14:18,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Or, your resistance is the pain.

169
00:14:18,892 --> 00:14:23,975
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you stop resisting, the non-gratified senses, they'll stop hurting.

170
00:14:24,827 --> 00:14:27,349
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't see that the pain is on your resistance level.

171
00:14:27,749 --> 00:14:32,972
[SPEAKER_00]: You think the pain is because there is no gratification of the senses, so I better go out and seek the gratification.

172
00:14:33,532 --> 00:14:37,195
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where you keep perpetuating that mistake that you yourself are living.

173
00:14:38,896 --> 00:14:49,002
[SPEAKER_00]: So first, you need to stop seeking out the gratification of the senses, so that your resistance to the non-gratifying senses becomes apparent as the cause of pain.

174
00:14:50,603 --> 00:14:53,605
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you realize, oh, I stop resisting, it stops hurting.

175
00:14:55,360 --> 00:14:57,202
[SPEAKER_00]: Gratifying senses becomes redundant.

176
00:14:57,602 --> 00:15:10,153
[SPEAKER_00]: The only reason you were gratifying it is because it was hurting beforehand, and you were mistakenly thinking the lack of gratification is what was hurting, so seeking gratification will remove the pain.

177
00:15:10,213 --> 00:15:10,333
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

178
00:15:10,814 --> 00:15:13,736
[SPEAKER_00]: Resistance of non-gratified senses, that's what's hurting.

179
00:15:14,117 --> 00:15:18,761
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the patīgā we spoke about, the natural patīgā of your senses, that you were resisting.

180
00:15:20,079 --> 00:15:23,741
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, habits of resistance... Well, yeah, the view of resistance.

181
00:15:24,461 --> 00:15:28,043
[SPEAKER_00]: That habit perpetuates the view, but the habit's there because of the view.

182
00:15:28,424 --> 00:15:36,648
[SPEAKER_00]: You still think the pain that you're experiencing is because of that, not because of your resistance to, to, to... pretty much to the pain you're experiencing.

183
00:15:39,770 --> 00:15:46,613
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why that view is important to... That's why without the right view, yeah, you won't go on.

184
00:15:46,693 --> 00:15:47,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving is the problem.

185
00:15:48,345 --> 00:15:56,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and that's why, like, craving that resistance results in upādāna, and upādāna feeds that resistance.

186
00:15:57,492 --> 00:15:59,074
[SPEAKER_00]: So, tantra feeds upādāna.

187
00:15:59,154 --> 00:16:00,835
[SPEAKER_00]: The view of upādāna is why tantra is there.

188
00:16:01,255 --> 00:16:02,296
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why it's beginningless.

189
00:16:02,376 --> 00:16:03,277
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a vicious circle.

190
00:16:03,357 --> 00:16:04,178
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no beginning.

191
00:16:04,238 --> 00:16:08,701
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not you are pure, then you develop tantra, then you develop upādāna.

192
00:16:08,922 --> 00:16:09,162
[SPEAKER_00]: No, no.

193
00:16:10,133 --> 00:16:16,215
[SPEAKER_00]: You start already infected, perverted, mistaken, confused, assumed.

194
00:16:16,975 --> 00:16:22,996
[SPEAKER_00]: Corruption is beginningless, but that's why if you understand it, if you uproot it, it cannot... it's irreversible.

195
00:16:23,716 --> 00:16:34,999
[SPEAKER_00]: So, once you remove that gratuitous assumption and... that's been feeding the craving and craving that's been feeding the assumption, once you fully uproot that, it cannot take place again.

196
00:16:36,159 --> 00:16:38,280
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it was never supposed to be there to begin with.

197
00:16:40,458 --> 00:16:42,570
[SPEAKER_00]: It was just a vicious circle perpetuating itself.

